{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Instructor Implementation Toolkit","title":"Prototype Showcase Logistics","description":"AIL 606 textbook section connecting prototype showcase logistics to prototype README traceability matrix connecting theory to screen changes, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"ail606-supplement-08-06-lo-01","statement":"Analyze how the prototype README traceability matrix connecting theory to screen changes shows a defensible decision in AIL 606"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-08-06-lo-02","statement":"Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-08-06-lo-03","statement":"Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-08-06-lo-04","statement":"Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next"}],"concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_instructor_implementation_toolkit","ail606_supplement_prototype_showcase_logistics","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":6,"top_tier_hardened":false,"course":"ail606-supplement","chapter":"08","section":"06","prereq_section_ids":["ail606-supplement/08/05"],"practice_ids":["ail606_supplement_8_6_prototype_showcase_logistics_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_8_6_prototype_showcase_logistics_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_8_6_prototype_showcase_logistics_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_8_6_prototype_showcase_logistics_calibration","ail606-supplement_08_06_lo_03_direct_check"],"lo_practice_map":{"ail606-supplement-08-06-lo-01":["ail606_supplement_8_6_prototype_showcase_logistics_calibration","ail606_supplement_8_6_prototype_showcase_logistics_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_8_6_prototype_showcase_logistics_ai_boundary"],"ail606-supplement-08-06-lo-02":["ail606_supplement_8_6_prototype_showcase_logistics_annotation_signal"],"ail606-supplement-08-06-lo-03":["ail606-supplement_08_06_lo_03_direct_check"],"ail606-supplement-08-06-lo-04":["ail606_supplement_8_6_prototype_showcase_logistics_annotation_signal"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Prototype Showcase Logistics\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Prototype Showcase Logistics, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_instructor_implementation_toolkit\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; repository license\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/08-06-prototype-showcase-logistics.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Prototype Showcase Logistics: Prototype Showcase Logistics, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA prototype showcase looks like an event-planning problem (tables, time, traffic) but it is really an assessment-design problem. The logistics you choose decide whether visitors leave having understood why each prototype made the design choices it did, or having only registered that the screens looked nice. The artifact at the center, a README traceability matrix linking each theoretical decision to the screen change it produced, only does its job if the format of the showcase prompts anyone to look at it.\n\nThis matters because the default format, an open house where visitors wander, optimizes for foot traffic and conversation volume, not for evidence exchange. Presenters repeat surface overviews, popular stations mob while others sit empty, and the reasoning that the whole capstone was meant to demonstrate never surfaces. Designing showcase logistics is the work of structuring attention so the deep evidence, the theory-to-screen rationale, is what visitors actually encounter.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the prototype README traceability matrix connecting theory to screen changes shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA program runs its end-of-term showcase as an open house: eight prototypes on tables, visitors free to roam, presenters answering whatever comes up. Within ten minutes three stations have crowds of six and two have nobody. Presenters, facing a churn of new arrivals, repeat the same thirty-second overview every couple of minutes and never reach the design reasoning. Visitors drift out having seen pretty screens and asked \"so what is it?\" The traceability matrices, painstakingly built, sit untouched on every table.\n\nThe case shows that logistics is not neutral plumbing; the format silently selected for milling over understanding. As you read, treat the showcase as an information environment whose structure determines what gets attended to, and ask what arrangement would make the theory-to-screen reasoning, not the visuals, the thing visitors take away.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nDesigning showcase logistics means deciding the format, timing, and prompts of a public demonstration so that the intended evidence is what audiences exchange. The central artifact is a traceability matrix: a table whose rows pair a design decision with the theory that justified it and the specific screen change that resulted, the same logic used in requirements engineering to prove that nothing important was dropped between intent and implementation. In a showcase the matrix is the deep content; logistics decide whether it gets read.\n\nTwo design principles govern the format:\n\n- **Define the unit of success as evidence exchanged, not visitors counted.** Foot traffic is easy to maximize and tells you nothing about understanding. The right metric is whether each visitor can recount at least one prototype's theory-to-screen link and whether presenters field substantive questions. The format should optimize for that, which usually means trading open milling for structure.\n- **Manage the audience's cognitive load with a focusing prompt.** A roaming visitor faces a blank-slate problem (\"what do I even ask?\") that defaults to the lowest-effort question, \"what is it?\" A single posted prompt, \"Ask me: which screen changed because of which theory?\", removes that load and routes attention to the matrix.\n\nTwo logistical levers turn the principles into a runnable plan: timed rotations (groups move every few minutes so every station gets visitors and presenters give their overview once per rotation rather than continuously) and staggered starts (groups begin at different stations so no prototype is mobbed while another is empty).\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"The section says the unit of success is evidence exchanged, not visitors counted. Why does optimizing for foot traffic actively work against the showcase's goal?\" answer=\"Foot traffic is easy to maximize but says nothing about understanding. Optimizing for it produces an open house where stations mob or empty, presenters repeat surface overviews to a churn of arrivals, and unprompted visitors default to the lowest-effort question, what is it. The deep evidence, the theory-to-screen reasoning, is exactly what that arrangement never surfaces.\"></self-explain>\n\n| Open-house default | Failure it produces | Structured alternative |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Visitors roam freely | Stations mob or sit empty | Timed rotations with staggered starts |\n| No conversation prompt | Default question is \"what is it?\" | Posted \"ask me about theory-to-screen\" card |\n| Success = attendance | Milling, no understanding | Success = evidence each visitor can recount |\n\n## Worked Example\n\nWalk through one concrete logistics decision for a prototype showcase: how visitors move through eight student prototype stations in a 50-minute session and how each station presents its README traceability matrix.\n\n**Before (the open-house plan).** The first plan was \"all eight prototypes open on tables, visitors wander, presenters answer questions.\" In the pilot, three popular stations had crowds of six while two stations had no visitors for 20 minutes, presenters repeated the same overview every two minutes (so no one heard the design reasoning), and visitors left without seeing any README traceability matrix because nothing prompted them to ask about it.\n\n1. **Name the real goal of the showcase.** The goal is not foot traffic; it is that each visitor can see at least a few prototypes' theory-to-screen reasoning, and each presenter gets substantive questions. The open-house format optimized for milling, not for evidence exchange.\n2. **Convert to timed rotations with a focusing prompt.** Visitors are grouped and rotate every 6 minutes (eight stations, two rounds covers the room). Each station posts one card: \"Ask me: which screen changed because of which theory?\" so the README matrix becomes the conversation, not an afterthought. By **[cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load)**, a single focusing prompt removes the \"what do I even ask\" load that left visitors silent.\n3. **Stagger start stations to balance load.** Groups start at different stations so no prototype is mobbed; presenter overview is given once per rotation, not continuously.\n\n**After (the showcase as an evidence exchange).** Timed 6-minute rotations, staggered starts, and a per-station \"ask me about theory-to-screen\" prompt. In the re-run every prototype received visitors in both rounds and presenters reported questions about specific README matrix rows rather than generic \"cool, what is it?\" The change is auditable: a reviewer can compare the open-house plan to the rotation schedule and the shift in the kind of questions presenters logged.\n\nWalk the design decisions a coordinator faces converting the failed open house. Trace where each choice sends visitor attention.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Will visitors leave understanding the reasoning?\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"Your pilot open house mobbed three stations and emptied two, and visitors left asking only so what is it. What do you fix first?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Add more tables and signage to move people through\",\"next\":\"traffic\",\"feedback\":\"This doubles down on throughput. More flow does not make anyone look at the traceability matrix.\"},{\"label\":\"Redefine success as evidence each visitor can recount\",\"next\":\"metric\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Reframing the metric rules out just add more tables and points you at attention, not volume.\"}]},\"metric\":{\"prompt\":\"With the metric reframed, an unprompted visitor still defaults to the lowest-effort question. What removes that blank-slate load?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Post a per-station card: ask me which screen changed because of which theory\",\"next\":\"prompt\",\"feedback\":\"Yes. A single focusing prompt routes attention to the matrix and upgrades the default question.\"},{\"label\":\"Give a prize for the most-visited prototype\",\"next\":\"prize\",\"feedback\":\"This rewards foot traffic, the wrong metric, and pulls attention further from the reasoning.\"}]},\"traffic\":{\"prompt\":\"The room is busier but presenters still log so what is it. What did throughput buy?\",\"feedback\":\"Energy, not understanding. A busy room is exactly what an open house over-produces; high attendance with zero recountable reasoning is a failed assessment dressed as a hit event.\",\"choices\":[]},\"prize\":{\"prompt\":\"The crowd concentrates on the prize favorite and half the stations sit empty. What happened to coverage?\",\"feedback\":\"Rewarding visits worsened concentration. The wrong metric starved the stations the audit was meant to reach.\",\"choices\":[]},\"prompt\":{\"prompt\":\"The card works at visited stations, but popularity still concentrates the crowd. What timing lever guarantees even coverage?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Timed six-minute rotations with staggered starts\",\"next\":\"win\",\"feedback\":\"Exactly. Rotations and staggered starts ensure every station gets substantive visits in both rounds.\"},{\"label\":\"Let visitors keep roaming freely\",\"next\":\"traffic\",\"feedback\":\"Free roaming reproduces the mob-and-empty pattern you started with.\"}]},\"win\":{\"prompt\":\"Post-event, presenters log questions about specific README matrix rows rather than generic curiosity. What does that signal?\",\"feedback\":\"The observable signal that logistics moved attention to the evidence. The showcase is now an assessment that happens to be public.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nDesign the format around the evidence you want exchanged, then add only the logistics that protect it. A showcase feature earns its place if it raises the chance a visitor encounters the theory-to-screen reasoning; if it only adds spectacle or throughput, cut it.\n\nTest any showcase plan against three questions:\n\n1. **Evidence-encounter check:** What in this plan makes a visitor look at the traceability matrix rather than just the screen? If nothing prompts it, the deep content will go unseen no matter how good it is.\n2. **Attention-load check:** Does the format hand visitors a focusing question, or does it leave them to invent one and default to \"what is it?\" An unprompted audience asks the shallowest available question.\n3. **Coverage check:** Does the timing guarantee every station gets substantive visits, or will popularity concentrate attention and starve the rest? Rotations and staggered starts exist to answer this.\n\nA plan that fails these is an event; a plan that passes them is an assessment that happens to be public.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A team is proud of an excellent traceability matrix and assumes a strong matrix will sell itself at the showcase. Why is this a misread?\" explanation=\"Even an excellent matrix is passive. Without a format that points visitors at it, it competes with the visually louder prototype screen and loses. Artifact quality does not substitute for logistics that route attention to it.\" options='[{\"text\":\"A passive matrix loses to the louder screen unless logistics route attention to it.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"A strong matrix guarantees substantive questions on its own.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Matrices only matter in online showcases.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Foot traffic will naturally find the best matrix.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common misread is that a busy room is a successful showcase. Crowding and chatter feel like success but are exactly what an open house over-produces; high attendance with zero recountable reasoning is a failed assessment dressed as a hit event. Energy is not understanding.\n\nA second misread is that a great traceability matrix sells itself. Even an excellent matrix is passive; without a format that points visitors at it, it competes with the visually louder prototype screen and loses. The quality of the artifact does not substitute for logistics that route attention to it.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nConvert the failed open house into an evidence exchange. Begin from the metric: success is that each visitor can recount one prototype's theory-to-screen link, not that the room was full. That reframing immediately rules out \"just add more tables.\"\n\nNext, fix the attention default. Post one card at each station, \"Ask me: which screen changed because of which theory?\", so the traceability matrix becomes the conversation rather than an afterthought. This is a cognitive-load move; it removes the blank-slate problem that produced \"so what is it?\"\n\nThen fix coverage with timing. Group visitors and rotate every six minutes, eight stations across two rounds, with groups starting at different stations so nothing mobs or empties. Presenters now give their overview once per rotation and spend the rest of each visit on substantive questions. The walkthrough is complete when a post-event check shows presenters logging questions about specific matrix rows rather than generic curiosity, which is the observable signal that logistics moved attention to the evidence.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader of this section evaluates a showcase by what visitors leave understanding, not by how lively it looked. The diagnostic move is to ask, for any proposed format, what question a visitor will actually ask and whether the answer touches the design reasoning.\n\nRead any showcase plan against these four questions:\n\n1. What concrete prompt or arrangement points a visitor at the traceability matrix rather than the screen?\n2. What is the default question an unprompted visitor will ask, and how does the format upgrade it?\n3. Does the timing guarantee even coverage, or will attention concentrate on a few stations?\n4. What observable signal (the kind of questions presenters log) would tell you the format worked?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong showcase artifact shows the before format, the failure it produced (mobbing, empty stations, surface questions), and the revised plan with its rotation schedule, focusing prompt, and success metric. A reviewer should be able to see that the change shifted presenter-logged questions from \"what is it?\" toward specific traceability-matrix rows.\n\nA strong rationale sounds like this: \"I replaced the open house with six-minute staggered rotations and a per-station focusing card, because the open format optimized for milling and starved half the stations. I rejected the suggestion to give prizes for the most-visited prototype, because rewarding foot traffic doubles down on the wrong metric and pulls attention further from the reasoning.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the showcase fully online, asynchronous, with visitors browsing recorded walkthroughs on their own time. Timed rotations and staggered starts are meaningless when there is no shared room, so those levers do not transfer. The constants are the metric (evidence recounted) and the attention principle (a focusing prompt), but the new constraint is that nothing forces a visitor past the screen recording into the matrix. The faithful transfer is to embed the focusing question into the artifact itself (open each walkthrough by answering \"which screen changed because of which theory?\") and to capture evidence-encounter through a short prompt visitors complete. Copying the in-person rotation schedule into an online setting would be planning traffic flow for a room that no longer exists.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the prototype README traceability matrix connecting theory to screen changes packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/08-06-prototype-showcase-logistics-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"prototype README traceability matrix connecting theory to screen changes\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"08.06\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the prototype README traceability matrix connecting theory to screen changes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the prototype README traceability matrix connecting theory to screen changes for AIL 606 08.06. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_prototype_showcase_logistics\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the prototype README traceability matrix connecting theory to screen changes records cognitive-load diagnosis, support request, accepted revision, rejected suggestion, and confidence change.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_instructor_implementation_toolkit\", \"ail606_supplement_prototype_showcase_logistics\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_8_6_prototype_showcase_logistics_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_prototype_showcase_logistics","stem":"A showcase plan is judged a success because 'the room was packed all session.' What is the strongest critique?","options":["It is a success; a packed room means engagement.","Foot traffic is not the goal; the goal is visitors seeing theory-to-screen reasoning and presenters getting substantive questions.","It would be better with even more visitors.","Success should be measured by how long visitors stayed."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A showcase is an evidence exchange; crowd size does not show that design reasoning was communicated.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_8_6_traffic_equals_success","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_prototype_showcase_logistics"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-08-06-lo-01"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_8_6_prototype_showcase_logistics_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"Visitors keep asking only 'cool, what is it?' and never see the README traceability matrix. What change surfaces the design reasoning?","options":["Make the prototypes look more impressive.","Post a per-station card: 'Ask me which screen changed because of which theory.'","Hand visitors a longer printed program.","Add background music to the room."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A focusing prompt makes the traceability matrix the conversation rather than an afterthought.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_8_6_no_focusing_prompt","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-08-06-lo-02","ail606-supplement-08-06-lo-04"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_8_6_p